Fuzzing what other people can send
==================================

Each target feeds libFuzzer's inputs to something that reads bytes or
text from someone else, and fails on a panic, a hang, running out of
memory, or a broken rule the target checks. Coverage-guided: inputs that
reach new code are kept (fuzz/corpus/<target>) and grown from.

Needs the nightly toolchain and cargo-fuzz (normal builds stay on
stable):

    rustup toolchain install nightly --profile minimal
    cargo install cargo-fuzz

From the top of the repository:

    cargo +nightly fuzz run <target>                       # until stopped
    cargo +nightly fuzz run <target> -- -max_total_time=600
    cargo +nightly fuzz run <target> fuzz/artifacts/<target>/crash-...   # one input again

The first build takes a few minutes (everything is built instrumented);
fuzz/target, corpus and artifacts aren't kept in git.

Targets
-------

frame         A frame from the wire (protocol::fuzz_frame): decodes or
              errors, and what decodes encodes back the same. Also a
              bundle's notices.
cover_slots   Cover slots one after another on one connection
              (cover::Inbound), and every item that comes out whole read
              as a frame.
video_pieces  A call's video pieces in any order, count and size
              (video::VideoDecoder::push): never more held than the caps
              (check_held), what's whole decoded in OpenH264.
h264, h265    The stream as the graphics-chip decoders read it before the
              chip does (media's h264 and h265: SPS, PPS, slice headers).
codes         Pasted or scanned text: a contact's code, a code for adding
              a device, a peers list.
mdns          A packet from anyone on the network (swarm_core::mdns):
              read as a query and as an answer.

`protocol::fuzz_frame` and `VideoDecoder::check_held` exist only in fuzz
builds (`#[cfg(fuzzing)]`, which cargo fuzz sets).

When it finds something
-----------------------

Fix it, then put the input in a test of its own in the crate it's in
(an ordinary stable test, as media/src/h265.rs
reference_picture_sets_too_long_are_refused_not_overflowed does
with media/testdata/h265-rps-steps.bin), so it stays fixed without the
fuzzer.

Not fuzzed: the Noise handshake and QUIC (snow's and quinn's), Opus (C,
libopus's), the vault (each line is AEAD-sealed: anything altered fails
to open before it's parsed).
